\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1. Research question\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 For each relevant elliptic curve in SEC 2, take its published base point 
\f2\fs26 G
\f1\fs24  and compute the unique half within the odd-order cyclic subgroup generated by 
\f2\fs26 G
\f1\fs24 :\
[\uc0\u8232 H = [(n+1)/2]G, \\qquad [2]H=G,\u8232 ]\
where 
\f2\fs26 n
\f1\fs24  is the (odd) order of 
\f2\fs26 G
\f1\fs24 . Then inspect the canonical nonnegative integer representative of the affine 
\f2\fs26 x
\f1\fs24  coordinate of 
\f2\fs26 H
\f1\fs24  and compare its bit length with the field degree / bit length. In binary fields the coordinate representation depends on the specified polynomial basis; the meaning of 'short' should be fixed before cross-curve comparisons.\
This is a 
\f0\b group-theoretic half
\f1\b0 , not ordinary integer halving of either coordinate. On a full curve with even group order, there may be other preimages of 
\f2\fs26 G
\f1\fs24  under doubling, but the subgroup half above is canonical.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

