\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 3. Mathematical interpretation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 For odd 
\f2\fs26 n
\f1\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 2
\f1\fs24  is invertible modulo 
\f2\fs26 n
\f1\fs24 , so 
\f2\fs26 k=(n+1)//2
\f1\fs24  satisfies 
\f2\fs26 2k \uc0\u8801  1 (mod n)
\f1\fs24 . Consequently 
\f2\fs26 H=kG
\f1\fs24  is uniquely determined within 
\f2\fs26 <G>
\f1\fs24 .\
Define 
\f2\fs26 b=bit_length(x(H))
\f1\fs24  using the canonical integer encoding of the field element, and 
\f2\fs26 m
\f1\fs24  as the coordinate width. A short coordinate has 
\f2\fs26 m-b
\f1\fs24  leading zero bits. If an x-coordinate were uniformly distributed over 
\f2\fs26 m
\f1\fs24 -bit strings, the rough probability of at least 
\f2\fs26 t
\f1\fs24  leading zeros would be 
\f2\fs26 2^-t
\f1\fs24 ; for real curves the exact distribution and conditioning matter. Testing many curves, multiples, encodings, or candidate definitions of 'short' introduces a look-elsewhere effect.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Security interpretation:
\f1\b0  a small 
\f2\fs26 x(H)
\f1\fs24  does not by itself reveal the discrete logarithm of 
\f2\fs26 G
\f1\fs24 , because the relation 
\f2\fs26 G=2H
\f1\fs24  is already public and trivial. It could nevertheless be interesting for generator provenance or deliberate parameter selection. Any stronger inference requires a specified null model and a comparison with the standards-generation process.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 4. SageMath verification code (NEW, NOT RECOVERED)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The following is a newly authored skeleton for reproducibility. It is 
\f0\b not
\f1\b0  presented as historical code from the emails.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 # SageMath: supply exact SEC 2 parameters for each curve.\
def analyze_prime_curve(p, a, b, gx, gy, n, label='curve'):\
    F = GF(ZZ(p))\
    E = EllipticCurve(F, [F(a), F(b)])\
    G = E(F(gx), F(gy))\
    assert n % 2 == 1\
    assert n * G == E(0)\
    H = ((ZZ(n) + 1) // 2) * G\
    assert 2 * H == G\
    x = ZZ(H[0])\
    width = ZZ(p).nbits()\
    return \{\
        'curve': label,\
        'half_x_hex': hex(x),\
        'half_x_bits': x.nbits(),\
        'field_bits': width,\
        'leading_zero_bits': width - x.nbits(),\
        'half_y_hex': hex(ZZ(H[1])),\
    \}\
\
# Example invocation, after inserting exact published values:\
# print(analyze_prime_curve(p, a, b, Gx, Gy, n, 'secp256k1'))\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 This function is for short-Weierstrass 
\f0\b prime-field
\f1\b0  SEC curves. Binary-field curves require separate construction and a documented polynomial basis. Curve parameters have deliberately not been invented or copied from an unverified source. Also verify that 
\f2\fs26 G
\f1\fs24  has order exactly 
\f2\fs26 n
\f1\fs24 , rather than relying only on 
\f2\fs26 n*G=0
\f1\fs24 , if parameters are untrusted.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

